# KNOWN GAP — RUST LANE. A comprehension over `String.bytes()` over-reads the
# 1-byte element slot, producing garbage from UNINITIALIZED memory at rc 0.
#
#     Vector[int] v = [b for b in s0.bytes()]     # prints 16961, not 65
#
# 16961 == 0x4241 -- two bytes ("AB") read out of a one-byte slot. Under
# `gg build --sanitize` the same program prints -4702111234475015615 ==
# 0xBEBEBEBEBEBE4241: ASan's 0xBE poison read alongside the two real bytes, so
# this is an UNINITIALIZED READ, not merely a wrong number. No crash, no
# sanitizer report -- the silently-wrong-values cell, which is the dangerous one.
#
# The STATEMENT form of the identical iteration is CORRECT (prints 65):
#
#     for b in s0.bytes():
#         v.push(b)
#
# so the discriminator is COMPREHENSION-vs-STATEMENT, and the mechanism is this
# round's headline class: the accumulator is minted from the SOURCE element
# (1 byte) rather than the RESULT element (i64).
#
# ⚠ DISTINCT from the filed sibling at TODO.md:922 on THREE axes: that one is
# the SELF-HOST lane, the STATEMENT form, and requires `.enumerate()` -- and its
# text explicitly records "Rust gg is correct", which this program falsifies for
# the comprehension form. Same 16961 magic number, same 2-byte over-read, but a
# different lane and a different construct.
#
# ⚠ AXIS: the RESULT-ELEMENT NARROW TYPE (u8 via .bytes()) is sampled by NO cell
# in the comprehension corpus -- every grid samples {int, String, float}. That is
# why this survived the round that fixed the same class for wide elements.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 2 then 65, exactly as the statement form does.
# Core #8 (a known wrong answer is a defect, not a parity excuse) + Core #12
# (an unsampled axis cell proves nothing about the sampled ones).
# Measured 2026-08-24 by R44 Track-K brief-review pass 5, orchestrator-verified.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    String s0 = mk("A", "B")
    Vector[int] v = [b for b in s0.bytes()]
    print(v.len())
    print(v.get(0).unwrap())
